.\logs\codex-20261027T2136388310238Z-ed956557521e4ee68b5c6d7e5658cc7f.log:9732:- `predecessor` has exactly `sequence` and `sha256`: initially integer 0 and null; thereafter the latest environment claim's integer sequence and exact-byte hash. Initially `owner_reconciliation=not_applicable`. After a claim, every new prerequisite approval binds that exact predecessor and expected DB state and is approved after the claim was written. Complete, consistent published evidence of verified success, verified no-op, or conclusive safe premutation failure permits `owner_reconciliation=not_applicable`; the proven continuation state must match the new expected start. Uncertain commits, postcommit audit failures, missing/incomplete evidence, contradictions, and unresolved drift require `owner_reconciliation=completed`. The owner must reconcile the outcome and verify database state under the approved lock/recovery procedure before making that exceptional attestation. Every successor still requires a distinct explicit single-use owner authorization; no existing record authorizes a retry or later no-op.
